  "pages": 18,
  "question": "For Erdős #243, when does a cubic growth rate force an irrational reciprocal sum, and what separate increment bound forces a Sylvester tail?",
  "precis": "Every strictly increasing sequence of positive integers with a_n²/a_{n+1}=1+3/n+o(n^{-3}) has an irrational reciprocal sum. A cubic profile for hypothetical rational-tail numerators is excluded by square specialisation and congruences modulo seven. The zero-indexed theorem has a Lean proof; the paper obtains the one-based statement by an ordinary finite-prefix argument. Separately, if a_{n+1}/a_n²→1 and the sum is rational, an eventual upper bound on increments of P_n/a_n, with P_n=∏_{j<n}a_j, forces the Sylvester recurrence eventually. That extra bound is not derived from growth and rationality alone; unrestricted Erdős #243 remains open.",
  "owns": "the cubic-rate irrationality theorem and the problem-specific exposition for Erdős #243: the centred integer state, negative-part exclusions, conditional Sylvester criterion, and remaining arithmetic estimate",
  "not_authority_for": "the validity of claims tagged Lean, which belongs to the cited kernel-checked source, or a solution to Erdős #243, which remains open",
